- *(a)* Modelled sensor electrical power (3 W overhead + point rate × electrical energy per point) for a 200 m, 10% extended target. The 3 W is an assumed fixed overhead for scanner motor, system SoC, heaters and power conversion, the same for both sensor types. Bands span the low/high parameter sets, and the yellow band marks the 8–25 W typical of automotive lidars.
- *Product markers* show published point rate and power. They are **at different ranges**, so they are sanity checks, not fits:
  - Hesai ATX: 3.84 Mpts/s, 8 W, 230 m at 10% [P];
  - Hesai ETX: ≈15 Mpts/s implied by field of view ÷ resolution × frame rate ($120^\circ/0.06^\circ=2{,}000$ columns × $20^\circ/0.027^\circ\approx740$ rows × 10 Hz ≈ 14.8 Mpts/s, §C3b), <13 W, 300 m at 10% [P/E];
  - LuminWave F1: 128 lines at 0.1° and 10 Hz, ≈1.5 Mpts/s implied ($120^\circ/0.1^\circ=1{,}200$ columns × 128 lines × 10 Hz = 1.54 Mpts/s over its 120° horizontal field, §C3b), <45 W, 250 m at 10% [P/E].
- *(b)* Central energy breakdown.

edits.new:
- *(a)* Modelled sensor electrical power (3 W overhead + point rate × electrical energy per point) for a 200 m, 10% extended target. The 3 W is an assumed fixed overhead for scanner motor, the sensor's system-on-chip (SoC), heaters and power conversion, the same for both sensor types. Bands span the low/high parameter sets, and the yellow band marks the 8–25 W typical of automotive lidars.
  - *Product markers* show published point rate and power. They are **at different ranges**, so they are sanity checks, not fits; subsection 1 derives the implied rates:
    - Hesai ATX: 3.84 Mpts/s (million points per second), 8 W, 230 m at 10% [P];
    - Hesai ETX: ≈15 Mpts/s implied, <13 W, 300 m at 10% [P/E];
    - LuminWave F1: 128 lines at 0.1° and 10 Hz, ≈1.5 Mpts/s implied, <45 W, 250 m at 10% [P/E].
- *(b)* Electrical energy per point in the central case, for ToF and FMCW in 2026 and 2032, split into the emitted optical energy, the loss in the laser, semiconductor optical amplifier (SOA) and driver, and the receiver electronics.
